(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-phases-of-sleep-quiz"]={"title":"Phases of Sleep Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which recording shows the atonia of voluntary muscles during rapid eye movement sleep?","choices":["Electromyogram","Electrooculogram","Electroencephalogram","Blood pressure recording"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The electromyogram, the muscle recording, shows atonia of voluntary muscles in rapid eye movement sleep, sparing the eye muscles and diaphragm, as inactivity due to inhibited alpha motor neurons. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 28"},{"q":"Which wave is seen in infancy and in very deep sleep?","choices":["Theta waves","Alpha waves","Beta waves","Delta waves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Delta waves, below 3.5 cycles per second with voltages 2 to 4 times greater than most other waves, occur in very deep sleep, in infancy, and in persons with serious organic brain disease. Deep sleep marked by delta is stage N3. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 25, 26"},{"q":"During sleep, the electroencephalogram shows high-voltage waves slower than 3.5 cycles per second. Which stage fits?","choices":["Stage N3","Stage N1","Stage N2","Rapid eye movement sleep"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Waves slower than 3.5 cycles per second with high voltage are delta waves, and delta waves mark stage N3, the deep, restful and deepest non-rapid eye movement stage, in which body and tissue growth and repair occur. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 25, 26, 27, 28"},{"q":"In an unconscious person, which observation points to coma rather than sleep?","choices":["Waves below 3.5 cycles per second","No arousal to any stimulus","Very depressed muscle tone","Slow heart rate, low blood pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Arousability separates the two states: sleep is unconsciousness from which a person can be aroused by sensory or other stimuli, and coma is unconsciousness from which a person cannot be aroused. Failure to rouse to any stimulus points to coma. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 10, 12, 14, 26, 28"},{"q":"Where are the reticular activating nuclei that start wakefulness located?","choices":["Thalamus","Frontal and parietal cortex","Mesencephalon and upper pons","Occipital cortex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The proposed model names the mesencephalic and upper pontine reticular activating nuclei. Released from inhibition, they activate spontaneously, excite the cerebral cortex and peripheral nervous system, and receive positive feedback from the cortex. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 22, 23, 26"},{"q":"Which state combines vivid dreaming, irregular heart and breathing rates, and very depressed muscle tone?","choices":["Rapid eye movement sleep","Non-rapid eye movement sleep","Quiet wakefulness","Alert wakefulness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep is the active, dreaming form of sleep: dreams are vivid, heart and breathing rates become irregular, and muscle tone is very depressed, with atonia of voluntary muscles except the eyes and diaphragm. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 10, 14, 27, 28"},{"q":"Which function of sleep concerns development of the nervous system?","choices":["Targeted erasure of synapses","Conservation of metabolic energy","Clearance of metabolic waste","Neural maturation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neural maturation is one of the functions of sleep and concerns the developing nervous system. The other functions named are facilitation of learning or memory, targeted synapse erasure, cognition, clearance of metabolic waste and conservation of metabolic energy. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What causes the muscle inactivity of rapid eye movement sleep?","choices":["Inhibited alpha motor neurons","Fatigued reticular activating neurons","Increased parasympathetic tone","Decreased peripheral vascular tone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The atonia of rapid eye movement sleep is inactivity due to inhibited alpha motor neurons, which matches the very depressed muscle tone that indicates inhibition of the spinal muscle control areas. The eye muscles and diaphragm are spared. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 12, 14, 19, 28"},{"q":"What shared electroencephalogram pattern makes rapid eye movement sleep hard to tell from alert wakefulness?","choices":["Waves of 4 to 7 cycles per second","Irregular, high-frequency waves","Rhythmical 8 to 13 cycles per second","High-voltage, low-frequency waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In rapid eye movement sleep the waves are irregular and high in frequency, beta waves like those of an awake, active person, which makes it often difficult to tell rapid eye movement sleep from alert wakefulness. Atonia of voluntary muscles is the difference. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 25, 26, 28, 29"},{"q":"After someone stays awake for more than 24 hours, what is sleep like in the first hour after falling asleep?","choices":["Rapid eye movement with vivid dreams","Light and drowsy throughout","Restless, with irregular breathing","Deep and extremely restful"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After more than 24 hours awake, the sleep in the first hour after going to sleep is deep, extremely restful non-rapid eye movement sleep, with blood pressure, heart rate, breathing rate and metabolic rate falling by 10 to 30%. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 10, 12, 28"},{"q":"Which wave runs below 3.5 cycles per second, has voltages 2 to 4 times greater than most other waves, and appears in serious organic brain disease?","choices":["Alpha waves","Beta waves","Theta waves","Delta waves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Delta waves include all waves below 3.5 cycles per second, often with voltages 2 to 4 times greater than other brain waves, and they occur in very deep sleep, in infancy and in persons with serious organic brain disease. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 25, 26"},{"q":"During which stage does body and tissue growth and repair occur?","choices":["Stage N2","Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N3","Stage N1"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stage N3, the deep, restful and deepest non-rapid eye movement stage marked by delta waves, is when body and tissue growth and repair occur. It is concentrated in the early cycles of the night and is also the stage that decreases with age. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 10, 28"},{"q":"Across a typical night, when is the deepest non-rapid eye movement sleep concentrated?","choices":["In the middle cycles","In the final cycles","Evenly through the night","In the early cycles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A plot of sleep stages across a night shows the deepest non-rapid eye movement stages in the first cycles, while later cycles stay lighter and their rapid eye movement periods lengthen. Deep, extremely restful sleep is also described in the first hour after going to sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 10, 12"},{"q":"Once wakefulness begins, what lets it sustain itself?","choices":["Ongoing activity of sleep centers","Fatigue of activating neurons","Positive feedback with the cortex","Negative feedback from the cortex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once wakefulness begins it can sustain itself naturally: the excited cerebral cortex sends positive feedback signals back to the mesencephalic reticular activating nuclei, which keeps them active until their neurons fatigue after many hours. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 19"},{"q":"When an awake, resting person directs attention to a specific mental task, what happens to the brain waves?","choices":["Alpha waves give way to beta waves","Beta waves give way to alpha waves","Alpha waves give way to theta waves","Alpha waves give way to delta waves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When an awake person's attention is directed to a specific type of mental activity, alpha waves stop and are replaced by beta waves, the asynchronous, low-voltage waves of an alert brain actively processing information. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24, 25, 26, 28"},{"q":"During non-rapid eye movement sleep, by about how much do blood pressure, heart rate, breathing rate and metabolic rate fall?","choices":["1 to 5%","10 to 30%","40 to 60%","70 to 90%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Non-rapid eye movement sleep is associated with decreased peripheral vascular tone and other vegetative bodily functions, with blood pressure, heart rate, breathing rate and metabolic rate falling by 10 to 30%. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 10, 12"},{"q":"Which function of sleep reduces the body's use of fuel?","choices":["Clearance of metabolic waste","Neural maturation","Targeted erasure of synapses","Conservation of metabolic energy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Conservation of metabolic energy is one of the functions of sleep, and in non-rapid eye movement sleep the metabolic rate falls by 10 to 30%. It sits beside neural maturation, learning, targeted synapse erasure, cognition and clearance of metabolic waste. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 17"},{"q":"Which circumstance can bring out theta waves in an awake adult?","choices":["Disappointment or frustration","Quiet rest with eyes closed","Focused mental activity","Very deep sleep"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Theta waves, 4 to 7 cycles per second, occur during emotional stress in some adults, especially during disappointment and frustration. They also appear in children's parietal and temporal regions and in many brain disorders, especially degenerative brain states. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 24, 25, 26, 28"},{"q":"About how often does a rapid eye movement episode recur during sleep?","choices":["About every 20 minutes","About every 45 minutes","About every 60 minutes","About every 90 minutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each rapid eye movement episode recurs about every 90 minutes, which matches sleep cycles of 90 to 120 minutes, 4 to 5 cycles in an average 8 hours of sleep. Each episode itself lasts 5 to 30 minutes. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 9"},{"q":"States such as sleep, wakefulness and extreme excitement arise mainly from what?","choices":["Changes in blood pressure","Signals from peripheral nerves","Forces generated within the brain","Stimuli from outside the body"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sleep, wakefulness, extreme excitement and different levels of mood are all states of brain activity, and for the most part each arises from activating or inhibiting forces generated within the brain itself, not from outside events. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 5, 12, 18"},{"q":"What happens in the deepest stage of non-rapid eye movement sleep?","choices":["Drowsy transition from wakefulness","Irregular heart and breathing rates","Body and tissue growth and repair","Vivid dreaming with an active brain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The deepest non-rapid eye movement stage, stage N3, is restful sleep marked by delta waves, and it is when body and tissue growth and repair occur. Its delta waves run below 3.5 cycles per second with high voltage, and the stage decreases with age. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 10, 14, 28"},{"q":"How do heart rate and breathing rate behave during rapid eye movement sleep?","choices":["They become irregular","They fall by 10 to 30%","They slow with parasympathetic tone","They rise steadily with metabolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In rapid eye movement sleep heart rate and breathing rate become irregular, which is characteristic of the dream state, alongside rapid eye movements and an active brain. Non-rapid eye movement sleep instead brings a slow heart rate and a decreased breathing rate. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 12, 14"},{"q":"Why is rapid eye movement sleep also called desynchronized sleep?","choices":["Neuron firing is uncoordinated","Dreams are not stored in memory","Waves are slow and high in voltage","Heart and breathing rates are regular"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep is called desynchronized sleep because there is a lack of synchrony in neuron firing during its brain activity, which gives irregular, high-frequency waves on the electroencephalogram. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 13, 26, 29"},{"q":"About what share of a young adult's sleep time does rapid eye movement sleep occupy?","choices":["About 10%","About 25%","About 50%","About 75%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep occurs in episodes and occupies about 25% of a young adult's sleep time, with an episode recurring about every 90 minutes and lasting 5 to 30 minutes. The majority of the sleep cycle is stage N2, a non-rapid eye movement stage. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 13, 28"},{"q":"Which stage is the drowsy transition from wakefulness into light sleep?","choices":["Stage N3","Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N1","Stage N2"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stage N1 is the transition from wakefulness: the person is somnolent or drowsy and in light sleep. It is the first, lightest non-rapid eye movement stage, and the electroencephalogram voltage becomes low. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 27, 28"},{"q":"Which wave does the electroencephalogram show in stage N2?","choices":["Delta waves","Sawtooth waves","Theta waves","Beta waves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stage N2, the less responsive, deeper sleep that is the longest stage by total duration, shows theta waves, 4 to 7 cycles per second, on the electroencephalogram. Delta waves wait for stage N3. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 25, 28"},{"q":"Which combination fits non-rapid eye movement sleep rather than rapid eye movement sleep?","choices":["Waves that resemble wakefulness","Low blood pressure, slow heart rate","Irregular heart and breathing rates","Brain metabolism up to 20% higher"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Non-rapid eye movement sleep brings increased parasympathetic tone with low blood pressure, a slow heart rate and a decreased breathing rate, part of a 10 to 30% fall in vegetative functions. In rapid eye movement sleep, heart and breathing rates become irregular instead. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 12, 14, 28"},{"q":"From which regions are beta waves recorded mainly?","choices":["Temporal and occipital regions","Parietal and frontal regions","Parietal and temporal regions","Occipital and parietal regions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta waves are recorded mainly from the parietal and frontal regions during specific activation of these parts of the brain, when a person is awake, alert and actively processing information. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 25"},{"q":"In which state does a mixed, low-voltage electroencephalogram with sawtooth waves appear?","choices":["Stage N2 sleep","Stage N3 deep sleep","Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N1 light sleep"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep shows a mixed, low-voltage electroencephalogram with sawtooth waves and beta activity similar to wakefulness, alongside irregular, sharply peaked eye movements and atonia of voluntary muscles. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 27, 28"},{"q":"Which wave is asynchronous and low in voltage, can reach 80 cycles per second, and appears when a person is actively processing information?","choices":["Theta waves","Beta waves","Alpha waves","Delta waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta waves occur at frequencies greater than 14 cycles per second and as high as 80, are asynchronous and low in voltage, and are recorded mainly from the parietal and frontal regions when the person is awake, alert and actively processing information. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24, 25, 26"}]};
